// AttackDatabase interface for fetching attack details
class AttackDatabase {
public:
    virtual std::map<std::string, AttackDetails> getAttacks(const std::string& characterType) const = 0;
};

// AttackDetails struct to store attack information
struct AttackDetails {
    std::string name;
    int cooldown;
};

// Global mutex for synchronizing access to the attack database
std::mutex dbMutex;

// Cooldown map to store the last usage time of each attack
std::map<std::string, std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point> cooldownMap;

// SelectAttack function signature
AttackDetails selectAttack(const AttackDatabase& db, const std::string& characterType);

// Implement the SelectAttack function
AttackDetails selectAttack(const AttackDatabase& db, const std::string& characterType) {
    // Lock the mutex to access the shared cooldown map safely
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(dbMutex);

    // Get the attacks for the given character type from the database
    auto attacks = db.getAttacks(characterType);

    // Iterate through the attacks and find one that is available to use
    for (const auto& attack : attacks) {
        // Check if the cooldown period has expired for this attack
        if ((std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - cooldownMap[attack.name]) >= std::chrono::seconds(attack.cooldown)) {
            // Update the last usage time of this attack in the cooldown map
            cooldownMap[attack.name] = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();

            // Return the selected attack
            return attack;
        }
    }

    // If no available attacks are found, randomly select an attack and set its cooldown to the specified period (9 seconds by default)
    auto it = attacks.begin();
    if (it != attacks.end()) {
        const auto selectedAttackName = it->first;
        cooldownMap[selectedAttackName] = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        return it->second;
    } else {
        // Log an error message if no valid attacks are found for the character type
        spdlog::error("No available attacks found for character type '{}'.", characterType);

        // Create a default attack with a 9-second cooldown period
        AttackDetails defaultAttack{ "DefaultAttack", 9 };
        return defaultAttack;
    }
}
```

**Design Choices:**

1. **Concurrency**: The function uses a global mutex to synchronize access to the shared cooldown map, ensuring thread safety in multi-threaded environments.

2. **Error Handling**: An error log message is written if no available attacks are found for the character type. You may want to consider integrating with a logging library that supports multiple log levels and provides additional features like file rotation or remote forwarding.

3. **Performance Optimizations**: To minimize memory usage, we use a map to store the cooldown information instead of individual variables for each attack. Additionally, the function is optimized for efficiency by checking only those attacks with non-expired cooldown periods, and selecting a default attack if no valid ones are found.

4. **Scalability**: The design allows for easy integration with an external attack database that can handle varying loads and support different character types with their specific cooldown periods. You may want to consider using a memory-efficient data structure like a hash map or a trie for the attack database, depending on your use case.

5. **Network Latency**: The function does not directly address network latency issues but can be integrated with an asynchronous HTTP library like libcurl to handle these concerns effectively. Ensure that the chosen library supports error handling and retries for failed requests.

