Here is a high-level design of the Java service class, utilizing popular libraries like `JsonSchema2POJO`, `Apache Kafka`, `Log4j2` for logging, and `Guava` for concurrent programming. This design follows best practices for scaling, error handling, and modularity.

```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.google.common.base.Preconditions;
import io.jsonschema.validator.util.JsonSchemaFactory;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class ChatService {
    private static final ObjectMapper objectMapper = new ObjectMapper();
    private static final JsonSchemaFactory jsonSchemaFactory = JsonSchemaFactory.byDefault();
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ChatService.class);

    public void processMessage(String payload, String roomId) {
        try {
            // Validate the incoming JSON against a schema
            Preconditions.checkNotNull(payload, "Payload cannot be null.");
            Preconditions.checkArgument(!payload.isEmpty(), "Payload must not be empty.");
            final var schema = jsonSchemaFactory.getJsonSchema("message_schema.json");
            final var message = objectMapper.readValue(payload, Message.class);

            // Validate user information and permissions
            UserValidator.validateUser(message.getUser());

            // Connect to the appropriate chat room based on the roomId
            final var chatRoomConnection = ChatRoomManager.getChatRoomConnection(roomId);

            // Forward the message to multiple users in the chat room
            for (var user : chatRoomConnection.getActiveUsers()) {
                forwardMessageToUser(message, user);
            }

        } catch (JsonProcessingException e) {
            log.error("Failed to process message due to JSON processing error.", e);
            // Log the failure and forward an error message back to the client

        } catch (IllegalArgumentException | UserNotAuthorizedException e) {
            log.error("Failed to process message due to validation errors or user permissions issues.", e);
            // Log the failure and forward an error message back to the client

        } catch (KafkaException e) {
            log.error("Failed to deliver message to chat room.", e);
            // Log the failure, attempt a retry with appropriate delay, and forward the message again

        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("Unexpected error occurred while processing message.", e);
            // Log the unexpected error and forward an error message back to the client
        }
    }

    private void forwardMessageToUser(final Message message, final User user) {
        // Use Apache Kafka for efficient message delivery
        final var producer = new KafkaProducer<>(kafkaConfig);
        final var key = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        final var value = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(message);

        producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("chat-" + user.getId(), key, value), (recordMetadata, e) -> {
            if (e != null) {
                log.error("Failed to deliver message to user.", e);
                // Log the failure and attempt a retry with appropriate delay
            } else {
                log.info("Delivered message to user: {}", user.getId());
                // Log successful delivery
            }
        });
    }
}
```

In this example, `Message`, `User`, `UserValidator`, and `ChatRoomManager` are assumed to be separate classes responsible for handling JSON processing, validation, user management, and chat room connections respectively. The actual implementation details of these classes will vary based on the specific requirements of your project.

